
  GET  /api/notifications?limit=50   -> { items[], unread_count, cursor }
  POST /api/notifications/read       { up_to_event_id }   -> advance cursor
  POST /api/notifications/:id/dismiss

  [1mUI.[0m A bell in the header with an unread count; clicking opens a dropdown of entries — actor, verb, task title,
  relative time — each linking to its task, plus "mark all read." Polling every 15s, [1mpaused while the tab is hidden and
[0m  [1mrefetched immediately on focus[0m. That last detail is worth the few lines: without it the badge is confidently wrong
  every time someone comes back from lunch.

  [1mFailure modes I'm designing for:[0m
  - [3mBackground poll fails[0m — retry with backoff, keep the last known state, show no error toast. A failed background
    request is not something to interrupt someone over. After several consecutive failures the badge goes muted to
    signal staleness rather than lying.
  - [3mUnknown event type[0m — stage three will add types stage two has never seen. The dropdown renders an unrecognized type
    as a generic line instead of throwing; a bell that crashes on a new event type is a bad way to find out.
  - [3mEvent referencing a deleted task[0m — the query joins [38;5;153mtasks[39m and drops orphans.
  - [3m[38;5;153mdismissed_events[39m growth[0m — prune rows older than the user's read cursor; once the cursor passes them they're
    redundant.

  [1mTesting[0m (unit only, per your choice). The derivation query is the highest-value target by a wide margin, so it gets
  table-driven tests against an in-memory SQLite: self-authored events excluded, non-participants excluded, cursor
  boundary conditions, dismissed exclusion, the collapsing window's edges, ordering and limit. The service layer gets
  tests that each mutation emits exactly one correct event and upserts participants, and that a rolled-back transaction
  leaves no orphan event.

  [1mThe honest gap:[0m skipping E2E leaves the polling and visibility behavior — badge accuracy across tab focus, dropdown
  rendering, mark-all-read round-tripping — with no automated coverage. That's a real hole in exactly the part users
  touch, and it'll need manual checking each time we change it. I think it's an acceptable trade for now given the app's
  size, and I'd revisit when stage three adds SSE, since connection lifecycle bugs are miserable to catch by hand.
  Flagging it so the choice stays visible rather than becoming an accident.

  If this looks right, I'll write it up as a spec.

